Fibrous Dysplasia (Pathological Fracture)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Dull localized bone pain, Shepherd's crook femoral deformity, Pathological fracture through weakened bone

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-023
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, immobilizing thigh pain following minimal weight-bearing in a patient with a known dysplastic skeletal condition.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young patient with known Fibrous Dysplasia who experienced a sudden, severe pain spike and an immediate inability to weight-bear on the affected leg following a minor movement. Urgent clinical assessment was strictly required to evaluate for an acute pathological fracture through a dysplastic lesion, exclude neurovascular compromise, and initiate rigid immobilization and potent analgesia prior to emergency transport or daytime orthopedic review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I just stood up to walk, and my thigh bone felt like it snapped with an explosion of sharp pain; I can't put any weight on it."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of monostotic Fibrous Dysplasia affecting the right proximal femur presents with an acute, severe pain crisis. Timeline: Baseline insidious, dull, localized bone pain over the past 6 months; patient walks with a known limp due to an asymmetrical bone deformity (Shepherd's crook bowing). Today, while simply pivoting on the right leg, the patient experienced a sudden onset of excruciating, sharp, localized pain in the mid-thigh. The patient collapsed and is entirely unable to weight-bear. The pain is severely exacerbated by any attempted movement of the hip or knee.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized on the floor; totally unable to weight-bear.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of displaced fracture and potential hemorrhage into the thigh compartment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, in extreme distress, actively guarding the right leg and refusing any manipulation.
Physical Exam: Right lower limb reveals an established, visible bowing deformity of the proximal femur. There is new, severe, localized, exquisite bony point tenderness over the mid-shaft of the femur, distinctly different from the baseline dull ache. Significant localized swelling is developing over the anterior thigh. Any micro-movement of the leg elicits extreme pain and muscle spasm. Crucially, distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are strong and symmetric; capillary refill is brisk (<2 sec). Sensation to light touch is completely intact in the foot and lower leg. No foot drop.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying securely on a carpeted floor; no trip hazards caused the event.
Medication Reconciliation: Baseline Paracetamol ineffective for the acute fracture pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Acute Pathological Fracture of the Right Femur secondary to Fibrous Dysplasia - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Osteomyelitis - Ruled out by the sudden, mechanical "snapping" onset of pain without preceding fever, rigors, or progressive localized erythema.

Soft Tissue Tear (Quadriceps/Hamstring) - Ruled out clinically by the deep, exquisite bony point tenderness on the femur itself rather than the muscle belly, and the context of a known underlying bony dysplastic lesion.

Femoral Artery Laceration - Ruled out clinically (at present) by the presence of strong, symmetric distal pulses, normal capillary refill, and absence of a rapidly expanding, pulsatile thigh hematoma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient, with highly vulnerable dysplastic bone architecture, presented with sudden, severe, localized pain and inability to weight-bear following minimal mechanical stress. This presentation is the hallmark of an acute pathological fracture through a weakened lesion. The patient was functionally stranded and in severe pain. Delaying clinical assessment was medically unacceptable due to the risk of fracture displacement, fat embolism, and unmanaged severe pain. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the clinical suspicion of fracture, excluded immediate limb-threatening vascular injury, and facilitated the immediate administration of high-dose parenteral analgesia and provisional splinting to stabilize the limb for urgent transfer to an orthopedic center.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg and Subcutaneous Morphine 5mg STAT for acute fracture pain management.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Remain absolutely still on the floor; do not attempt to drag yourself or the leg into a more comfortable position. Consequence: Prevention of the sharp, broken bone edges grinding together or slicing into surrounding blood vessels, preventing agonizing pain spikes and severe internal bleeding (negative reinforcement).
Action: Allow the caregiver to place heavy rolled towels on either side of the leg. Consequence: Creation of a physical block that stops the leg from rolling outward, significantly reducing the deep, throbbing muscle spasms trying to protect the break (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediately activated emergency medical services (000) for ambulance transfer to the nearest Emergency Department.
DME/Safety (Optional): Applied improvised lateral support (rolled towels) to prevent external rotation of the limb while awaiting transport.
Follow-up: Urgent orthopedic surgery admission for plain radiography (expecting expansile ground-glass lesions with cortical breach) and definitive surgical fixation.

